<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Touro Synagogue</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1763">1763</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Touro Synagogue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newport-rhode-island">Newport, Rhode Island</a>, is the oldest surviving synagogue building in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, dedicated in 1763. Designed by Peter Harrison for the Sephardic congregation Jeshuat Israel, it reflects the religious freedom of the Rhode Island colony. In 1790 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> wrote to its congregation promising that the United States would give 'to bigotry no sanction, to persecution no assistance'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/touro-synagogue.jpg" alt="Touro Synagogue" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kenneth C. Zirkel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Touro_Synagogue,_Newport,_Rhode_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Fifteen Spanish and Portuguese Jewish families reached Newport in 1658, probably from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> or British West Indies, by way of communities such as Curaçao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>. They followed the Spanish and Portuguese liturgy and for more than a century worshipped in rooms of private homes, though they bought and dedicated a cemetery in 1677. The synagogue was built between 1759 and 1763 under the leadership of the cantor Isaac Touro, a Dutch-born rabbi, and dedicated on 2 December 1763. Aaron Lopez, a Portuguese-born merchant and the wealthiest man in Newport, whose trade included candles, whale oil, rum and enslaved people, laid the cornerstone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The building is held to be the most notable work of the British architect Peter Harrison, an immigrant to British America who lived in Rhode Island. It faces east toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, with the ark of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> on the east wall. Twelve Ionic columns, each carved from a single tree and standing for the twelve tribes of Israel, carry the balconies. Above the ark a mural of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-commandments">Ten Commandments</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> was painted by Benjamin Howland, Newport's city clerk from 1825 to 1875. It stands at 72 Touro Street and remains an active <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> synagogue.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the late 18th century the community sent its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> scrolls and the deed to Congregation Shearith Israel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> for safekeeping, and the keys passed to the Goulds, a Quaker family. The building opened only now and then from the 1850s, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> immigrants from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> revived Jewish life and regular worship resumed in 1883. Judah Touro, son of Isaac, left $10,000 for the synagogue and cemetery.</p><p>The congregation reads Washington's letter every year with invited speakers. The site became a National Historic Site in 1946. A dispute over the building began in 2012 when the Newport congregation offered its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> rimonim to the Museum of Fine Arts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>; in 2017 a federal appeals court awarded ownership to Shearith Israel, and in 2019 the Supreme Court declined the case.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Touro Synagogue” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jeshuat Israel congregation dedicated the <b>Touro Synagogue</b> in Newport, Rhode Island, designed by Peter Harrison and built from 1759 under the leadership of Isaac Touro.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The royal charter of 1663, obtained by John Clarke, guaranteed full liberty in religious concerns, and the colony became a home for Baptists, Quakers and Jews, whose <b>Touro Synagogue</b> at Newport opened in 1763.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-rhode-island-and-providence-plantations">Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations</a></li></ul></section>
